    "title": "Troubleshooting VPC Network Peering Transitivity Limits",
    "scenario": "Company A connects VPC-1 to VPC-2 via VPC Network Peering. They also connect VPC-2 to VPC-3 via VPC Network Peering. Workloads in VPC-1 can communicate with VPC-2, and VPC-2 can communicate with VPC-3. However, workloads in VPC-1 cannot reach VPC-3. What is the architectural reason for this behavior?",
    "keywords": [
      "VPC Network Peering",
      "Non-Transitive Routing",
      "Network Connectivity Center",
      "Cloud VPN",
      "VPC Architecture"
    ],
    "isMultiSelect": false,
    "expectedSelectCount": 1,
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "VPC Peering routes have timed out and need a reboot.",
        "isTrap": true,
        "trapType": "manual_scripting_overhead"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Google Cloud VPC Network Peering is strictly non-transitive; traffic from VPC-1 cannot transit through VPC-2 to reach VPC-3. To enable communication, you must peer VPC-1 directly to VPC-3 or use Network Connectivity Center / HA VPN hub-and-spoke.",
        "isTrap": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "The MTU of VPC-1 is set to 1460 while VPC-3 is 1500.",
        "isTrap": true,
        "trapType": "premature_optimization"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "VPC-2 must have IP forwarding enabled on all its subnets.",
        "isTrap": true,
        "trapType": "public_ip_security_risk"
      }
    ],
    "correct": "B",
    "explanation": "VPC Network Peering in Google Cloud is strictly non-transitive. Only directly connected peering networks exchange routes. To route between VPC-1 and VPC-3, they must either peer directly, use Shared VPC, or utilize Network Connectivity Center / VPN gateways.",
    "distractors": {
      "A": "VPC Peering routes are managed by Google's SDN (Andromeda) and do not time out or require reboots.",
      "B": "Correct. Non-transitivity is a fundamental design rule of Google Cloud VPC Peering.",
      "C": "MTU differences cause packet fragmentation, not total route non-advertisement.",
      "D": "IP forwarding is a VM-level setting for NAT/router appliances, not a VPC subnet transitivity toggle."
    },
    "architectureComponents": [
      "VPC Network Peering",
      "Non-Transitive Routing",
      "Network Connectivity Center"
    ],
    "terraformSnippet": "resource \"google_compute_network_peering\" \"peering_1_3\" {\n  name         = \"vpc1-to-vpc3\"\n  network      = google_compute_network.vpc1.self_link\n  peer_network = google_compute_network.vpc3.self_link\n}",
    "officialDocUrl": "https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/vpc-peering#non-transitive-peering",
